      "index": 1,
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 30.85,
      "text": "You search for money back, but the best page only says refund. It still appears because semantic search usually converts your query and each document chunk into vectors: lists of numbers that capture patterns in meaning. An embedding model learns this useful geometry from patterns in large collections of training text. The query and the refund page land close together, while an unrelated banana page lands far away. Search compares those positions and returns the nearest useful chunks. That is embeddings in practice: matching meaning instead of exact wording. What search result has felt like it read your mind?",
